Here's how to implement global error handling with `IExceptionHandler` — no try-catch needed in endpoints:

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourNamespace.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Default handler returns 500 with no sensitive details

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

// ... after building the app:

app.UseExceptionHandler();  // Catches unhandled exceptions globally
app.UseStatusCodePages();   // Ensures non-exception error codes also use Problem Details
```

**How it works:**

- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the default handler to return RFC 7807 JSON for unhandled exceptions (500) — with no stack trace or message leaked.
- Your custom `ApiExceptionHandler` runs first. Known exceptions get mapped to specific status codes. Unknown ones (`return false`) fall through to the default 500 handler.
- `UseStatusCodePages()` ensures bare status codes (e.g., from authorization) also produce Problem Details.

**Example 500 response** (unhandled exception):
```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.6.1",
  "title": "An error occurred while processing your request.",
  "status": 500
}
```

**Example 404 response** (KeyNotFoundException):
```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Not Found",
  "instance": "/api/products/999"
}
```

This requires .NET 8+. The middleware catches exceptions from all endpoints globally — no per-endpoint try-catch needed.
